1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,186
Every server on a network is a house with a lot of doors. Most should be locked.

2
00:00:05,206 --> 00:00:07,709
The firewall decides which doors are open,

3
00:00:07,729 --> 00:00:10,833
and an open door you forgot is how attackers get in.

4
00:00:11,303 --> 00:00:16,531
In this video: restrict network access using firewall D and firewall C M D.

5
00:00:21,051 --> 00:00:23,634
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:24,104 --> 00:00:28,649
Restrict network access using firewalld and firewall-cmd.

7
00:00:29,119 --> 00:00:33,262
We'll start the firewall, see what is allowed, allow a service and a port,

8
00:00:33,282 --> 00:00:36,694
remove a service, and make it permanent, even after a reboot.

9
00:00:44,214 --> 00:00:46,766
Firewall D is the firewall service.

10
00:00:46,786 --> 00:00:50,588
Firewall dash C M D is the command that controls it.

11
00:00:50,608 --> 00:00:55,219
Anything not allowed is blocked. You allow traffic in two ways:

12
00:00:55,239 --> 00:01:00,805
by service name, like http or ssh, which is a named port, or by port number,

13
00:01:00,825 --> 00:01:03,671
like eight zero eight zero slash T C P.

14
00:01:12,141 --> 00:01:16,252
There are two copies of the rules. The running rules, active now.

15
00:01:16,272 --> 00:01:18,857
And the permanent rules, saved for later.

16
00:01:18,877 --> 00:01:23,242
With the option dash dash permanent, you change only the saved rules.

17
00:01:23,262 --> 00:01:27,055
Then dash dash reload copies them into the running firewall.

18
00:01:35,525 --> 00:01:40,055
Before the demo, silently, the lab installs the firewall D package,

19
00:01:40,075 --> 00:01:43,111
because this practice image does not have it.

20
00:01:43,131 --> 00:01:48,475
A normal R H E L system has it installed already. This step changes the system.

21
00:01:56,995 --> 00:01:59,842
First, is it running? Systemctl is active:

22
00:01:59,862 --> 00:02:04,280
inactive. Enable dash dash now starts it and turns it on at boot.

23
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:09,468
Then firewall dash C M D dash dash state: running. The first line is normal:

24
00:02:09,488 --> 00:02:12,335
it waits for the connection to the system.

25
00:02:23,855 --> 00:02:28,811
What is allowed now? Dash dash list dash services. Three services:

26
00:02:28,831 --> 00:02:33,862
cockpit, the web console, dhcp v six client, and ssh, remote login.

27
00:02:42,382 --> 00:02:44,527
Allow the web service, http.

28
00:02:44,547 --> 00:02:50,481
Dash dash permanent, dash dash add dash service equals http. It says success.

29
00:02:50,501 --> 00:02:56,975
But list the services again: http is not there yet. We only changed the saved rules.

30
00:03:08,495 --> 00:03:11,307
Reload. Now list again: http is allowed.

31
00:03:11,327 --> 00:03:15,131
The saved rules were copied into the running firewall.

32
00:03:23,651 --> 00:03:29,390
Now a port. Dash dash add dash port equals eight zero eight zero slash T C P.

33
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:33,952
Permanent again, then reload, then dash dash list dash ports:

34
00:03:33,972 --> 00:03:36,495
eight zero eight zero slash T C P.

35
00:03:46,015 --> 00:03:48,108
Restricting means removing too.

36
00:03:48,128 --> 00:03:52,812
Dash dash remove dash service equals cockpit, permanent, then reload.

37
00:03:52,832 --> 00:03:54,720
The list now has no cockpit.

38
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:06,676
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

39
00:04:08,196 --> 00:04:12,784
We reboot. The services and the port are still there, because we made them permanent.

40
00:04:20,304 --> 00:04:26,184
Your turn. Allow the service https, and open port five thousand slash T C P.

41
00:04:26,204 --> 00:04:29,057
Remove the service dhcp v six client.

42
00:04:29,077 --> 00:04:35,345
Make everything permanent, reload, run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

43
00:04:44,815 --> 00:04:46,566
Try before you open the solution.

44
00:04:52,086 --> 00:04:55,927
Next up: create, delete, and modify local user accounts.

45
00:04:56,397 --> 00:04:59,300
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
